Usage: gate-inbox [command]

Run the interactive manager when no command is given.

Gate Inbox runs your session beside the user's other agents and terminals.
Every command acts as the session it runs in, so run them from your own shell.

Agent sessions
  gate-inbox sessions [--parent <id|me>] [--status <state>] [--include-archived] [--limit <n>] [--json]
      list every agent session with its id, CLI, group, directory and status; call it before delegating anything
  gate-inbox spawn [--name <name>] [--prompt <text>] [--tool <cli>] [--model <model>] [--group <path>] [--directory <path>] [--nest] [--json]
      start another agent CLI on a task of its own, so independent work runs beside you instead of queued behind you
  gate-inbox send <session-id> "<message>" [--subject <label>] [--interrupt] [--as-human] [--json]
      queue a message for another agent; it is typed in once that agent is at rest, so it never lands on an approval prompt
  gate-inbox read <session-id> [--since <cursor>] [--json]
      read what another agent is doing: its status, any question it is holding, its last words, and either its whole screen or only what it has added --since a previous read's cursor
  gate-inbox send-children "<message>" [--json]
      queue one message for every session you spawned, for a correction about the work rather than about one worker
  gate-inbox place <session-id> [--release] [--json]
      file a session under this one when a spawn of yours landed with no parent, or --release one of your own children back to the top level
  gate-inbox answer <session-id> "<answer>" [--json]
      answer a question one of the sessions you spawned has stopped on, a question at a time; send queues a message and a session on a dialog never reads it
  gate-inbox wait [<session-id>...] [--children] [--until <state>] [--timeout <duration>] [--json]
      park until a session stops working, instead of reading its screen in a loop; name several or pass --children to park on a whole fan-out and return on the first one to arrive; exits non-zero when none of them did
  gate-inbox message-status <message-id> [--json]
      check whether a message you sent is queued, held, delivered, dropped or answered
  gate-inbox kill <session-id> [--json]
      stop another agent's process, ending whatever it is doing; its row keeps the last screen
  gate-inbox revive <session-id> [--json]
      bring a dead session back on its old row, resuming the conversation it held
  gate-inbox migrate <session-id> --tool <cli> [--name <name>] [--json]
      move a session's conversation to another agent CLI: a new session there reads the source's transcript and carries on; the source stays until you archive it
  gate-inbox archive <session-id> [--restore] [--json]
      file a finished session out of the active list, ending it if it is still running, or restore it with --restore; a row left archived is deleted for good after 7 days
  gate-inbox park [--dry-run] [--json]
      stop every live agent session the manager started and record the set, so the machine can reboot; --dry-run only prints the plan; runs from any shell
  gate-inbox unpark [--json]
      bring back every session park stopped, resuming the conversation each held; runs from any shell
  gate-inbox groups [--json]
      list the groups sessions and terminals are filed under
  gate-inbox create-group <path> [--directory <path>] [--json]
      create a group so a fleet you spawn stays together in the user's list
  gate-inbox delete-group <path> [--json]
      remove a group whose work is done; sessions still in it move to the root rather than stopping

Shared task list
  gate-inbox task <list|create|claim|finish|release|delete>
      the work list every session in this manager claims from
  gate-inbox task list [--json]
      read the work list every session shares: what is pending, who holds what, and what is blocked
  gate-inbox task create <title> [--body <text>] [--depends-on <id,id>] [--json]
      put a piece of work on the shared list so any session can pick it up
  gate-inbox task claim [<task-id>] [--json]
      take a task before you start it; omit the id to take the oldest pending task nothing is blocking
  gate-inbox task finish <task-id> [--json]
      mark a task you claimed as done, which unblocks everything waiting on it
  gate-inbox task release <task-id> [--json]
      hand a task you claimed back to the list for another session
  gate-inbox task delete <task-id>
      remove a task nobody needs any more

File reservations
  gate-inbox reserve <path>... [--mode exclusive|shared] [--note <text>] [--ttl <duration>] [--json]
      declare the files you are about to edit, so another agent in the same checkout finds out before both of you change them
  gate-inbox release-files [<path>...] [--json]
      give the leases back once the edits are made; name no path to release everything you hold
  gate-inbox reservations [--json]
      see which files the other sessions are working on right now

Managed terminals
  gate-inbox terminal <list|create|send|read|close>
      shells the user watches beside the agents, for work they should be able to watch, attach to or take over
  gate-inbox terminal list [--json]
      find a running terminal to reuse before opening another one
  gate-inbox terminal create [--group <path>] [--directory <path>] [--nest=false] [--json]
      open a terminal for work the user should see, such as SSH into a host; it hangs under this session unless --nest=false
  gate-inbox terminal send <terminal-id> --command "<text>" | --keys <key,key> [--json]
      run a command there, or send exact tmux keys such as C-c to control what is running
  gate-inbox terminal read <terminal-id> [--json]
      read what that terminal's screen currently shows
  gate-inbox terminal close <terminal-id>
      close a terminal opened under this session once its job is done, killing the pane and deleting the row

Your own session
  gate-inbox rename "<name>"
      name this session for the broad feature it is about, once, while it still carries a placeholder name

How much this session matters
  gate-inbox priority <urgent|high|medium|low|none>
      declare the tier of the work in this session, so triage hands it over ahead of the sessions that matter less

Options:
  -h, --help     Show this help text
  -v, --version  Print the installed version
  --log-path     Print where the diagnostic log is written (alias: logs)

